From: baseband lane
Subject: 0537 pandora_mpss: stop pointing INIT_IMAGE at other subsystems' ids

Three of this file's lists hand TrustZone an INIT_IMAGE carrying THIS image's metadata, and all three
had grown to include ids belonging to subsystems this kernel does not load:

  mp_probe_pas_ids   {4, 38, 24, 28, 34, 15, 33, 63, 0}   -> {4, 38}
  mp_probe_cross     {4, 38, 13, 36}   (0534 widened it)  -> {4, 38}
  mp_shutdown_first  {4, 38, 13}                          -> {4, 38}

That was not harmless.  TrustZone ACCEPTS INIT_IMAGE for pas 13 -- the ADSP's own id, and rings from
before the ADSP driver was switched off show `pas 13 init_image -> a0 0`.  So this kernel was setting
up a PIL state for a subsystem it never loads, on every boot with the probes on.  On the same volume,
minutes apart on 2026-11-23, another lane's image reached DESKTOP_OK while this lane's boot produced
LOGO_LOOP with hfs_mark_inconsistent 0, restart type 1 and no panic -- i.e. the volume and the kernel
were fine and the graphics stack still crash-looped.  A wrong PIL state for the ADSP is the best
explanation available, and it is this file's doing.

The first list's question ("which id does TrustZone know") stays answered by the PAS_IS_SUPPORTED
probe, which only queries and changes nothing -- that one keeps all ids.  The second list's question
("is the refusal about the id or the image") is answered: pas 13 is accepted, so it is not the id.
The third list's "control" shut a live subsystem down and returned the same b8 for every id.

A subsystem this kernel does not own is not a control group.

diff --git a/bsd/dev/pandora_mpss.c b/b/bsd/dev/pandora_mpss.c
index 2bdca6f..87678e1 100644
--- a/bsd/dev/pandora_mpss.c
+++ b/bsd/dev/pandora_mpss.c
@@ -993,7 +993,20 @@ int pandora_pas_init_only(const char *prefix, const char *base, unsigned pas, ui
 static void
 mp_probe_pas_ids(void)
 {
-	static const unsigned ids[] = {4, 0x26, 0x18, 0x1c, 0x22, 0x0f, 0x21, 0x3f, 0};
+	/*
+	 * Only the modem's own ids go through INIT_IMAGE here.
+	 *
+	 * This list used to be nine ids wide -- 15 (the IPA), 24, 28, 33, 34, 36 (the ADSP), 63 and 0 --
+	 * because the question at the time was "which id does TrustZone know".  That question is
+	 * answered and stays answered by the PAS_IS_SUPPORTED probe below, which only queries.  What
+	 * this loop does is different: it hands TrustZone an INIT_IMAGE carrying THIS image's metadata
+	 * under an id belonging to some other subsystem -- and for pas 13 (the ADSP) TrustZone accepts
+	 * it (`pas 13 init_image -> a0 0` appears in rings from before the ADSP driver was switched
+	 * off).  That is a live PIL state for a subsystem this kernel is not loading, and the best
+	 * explanation for this lane's boots producing LOGO_LOOP while another lane's image reaches
+	 * DESKTOP_OK on the same volume minutes earlier.
+	 */
+	static const unsigned ids[] = {4, 0x26};
 	if (!mp_option("pandora_mpss_pas_probe")) {
 		return;
 	}
@@ -1054,13 +1067,20 @@ mp_probe_cross(void)
 	 * Whichever way it comes out, the answer stops the guessing: until now every cross was inside
 	 * {4, 38} and so could not separate the two.
 	 */
-	static const unsigned ids[] = { MP_PAS, MP_PAS_DTB, MP_PAS_CONTROL, MP_PAS_ADSP1 };
+	/*
+	 * The modem's own ids only, for the same reason as the probe above: 0534 widened this to pas
+	 * 13 and 36 to separate "the id is refused" from "the image is refused", and that question is
+	 * worth asking once, not on every boot -- the answer in hand is that pas 13 is ACCEPTED, i.e.
+	 * the id is not the refusal.  Sending the modem's metadata under the ADSP's id again would
+	 * keep touching a subsystem this kernel does not load.
+	 */
+	static const unsigned ids[] = { MP_PAS, MP_PAS_DTB };
 
 	if (!mp_option("pandora_mpss_pas_probe2")) {
 		return;
 	}
-	printf("PANDORA MPSS: crossing 2 images with %u PAS ids (4, 38, 13, 36), each with its own metadata\n",
-	    (unsigned)(sizeof(ids) / sizeof(ids[0])));
+	printf("PANDORA MPSS: crossing %u images with %u modem PAS ids, each with its own metadata\n",
+	    (unsigned)(sizeof(images) / sizeof(images[0])), (unsigned)(sizeof(ids) / sizeof(ids[0])));
 	for (unsigned i = 0; i < sizeof(images) / sizeof(images[0]); i++) {
 		for (unsigned j = 0; j < sizeof(ids) / sizeof(ids[0]); j++) {
 			uint64_t answer = 0;
@@ -1087,12 +1107,16 @@ static void
 mp_shutdown_first(void)
 {
 	/*
-	 * The third id is a CONTROL, not a target: the ADSP's 13 is accepted by INIT_IMAGE in the same
-	 * boot this runs in (logs/1795302306 and 1795302772 show `pas 13 init_image -> a0 0`), so its
-	 * answer to PAS_SHUTDOWN says whether 0xffcfffb8 means "this id may not be touched" or merely
-	 * "that peripheral is not running".
+	 * The modem's own two ids, and nothing else.
+	 *
+	 * This list carried the ADSP's 13 as a "control": TrustZone accepts INIT_IMAGE for 13 in the
+	 * same boot (logs/1795302306 and 1795302772 show `pas 13 init_image -> a0 0`), so its answer to
+	 * PAS_SHUTDOWN was meant to say whether 0xffcfffb8 means "this id may not be touched" or
+	 * "that peripheral is not running".  The answer came back: b8 for every id, so the control
+	 * taught nothing -- while actually SHUTTING THE ADSP DOWN is not a neutral act.  A subsystem
+	 * this kernel does not own is not a control group.
 	 */
-	static const unsigned ids[] = { MP_PAS, MP_PAS_DTB, MP_PAS_CONTROL };
+	static const unsigned ids[] = { MP_PAS, MP_PAS_DTB };
 
 	if (!mp_option("pandora_mpss_shutdown_first")) {
 		return;
